  "query_preview": "(Today through Thursday)...\nIssued at 301 AM EDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nSurface high pressure is building southwestward into Indiana, which \nhas allowed stratus to diminish across the region. However, high \ncirrus is increasing due to a system over the Great Plains. Though \nthis system remains to our west, high and eventually mid-level cloud \ncover could become thick at times this afternoon and evening. A few \nlight rain showers cannot be ruled out either.\n\nThe upper-level pattern is blocked, with deep cut-off troughing over \nthe eastern seaboard. This will keep the system over the Plains to \nour west, even causing it to retrograde somewhat. High pressure \ncontinues to build westward bringing clearing skies late Friday into \nthe weekend. Patchy fog is possible at times Friday night and \nSaturday night, with ideal radiative cooling potential expected. \nLocally dense fog is possible as well.\n\nTemperatures are expected to rebound towards normal values (low to \nmid 70s), with lows being a bit cooler than normal (mid 50s). Expect \nhighs in the low to mid 70s with nightly lows in the upper 40s to \nlow 50s.\n\nNEXT WEEK\n\nGuidance has trended away from a strong, wrapped up mid latitude low \non Tuesday/Wednesday and more towards a weak wave passing to our \nnorth. Cloud cover and precipitation chances still increase, but \nstrong thunderstorm potential has decreased. Ensembles have diverged \nregarding the overall pattern late next week, with some models \nshowing a secondary wave developing Thursday or Friday. This wave \nmay interact with moisture from Hurricane Polo, currently off the \nsouthern coast of Mexico. Given high model spread, forecast \nconfidence is low. PoPs will be capped at chance for a broad \ntimeframe...late Tuesday through Friday. This will be refined as \nguidance comes into better agreement.",
